{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Correction to my disk-measurement comment above.\n\nI cited `docker_unaccounted_bytes` (2.03 -> 3.79 GB in 40 minutes) as showing that \"the storage growth is escaping through the space the container-layer and scratch caps close, not through the BuildKit budget already installed by S0/S6\". I withdraw that inference.\n\nThere is an open p2 bug on that metric — `regretful-enormous-horsefly` — measured on this host today. `docker_unaccounted_bytes` differences an ALLOCATED-BLOCKS `du -sx --block-size=1 /var/lib/docker` against APPARENT-size `docker system df` rows. Across ~1.88M files on 4 KiB xfs blocks that inflates the minuend ~12.4%, and the inflation scales with the tree. Measured split of a 2,685,139,712-byte residue: 1,501,924,837 bytes (56%) is xfs block-allocation overhead, only 1,183,214,875 bytes (44%) is real un-ledgered content. So most of the level, and plausibly most of the delta, is an artifact rather than bytes.\n\nWhat that changes for this epic's assessment, precisely:\n\n- The claim that growth is escaping specifically through the writable-layer/scratch gap is NOT supported by the evidence I gave for it. It may still be true — S5's own report is explicit that a running container's writable layer is unbounded and unreclaimable — but I had not measured it, and the number I used cannot establish it.\n- The claim that the BuildKit cap IS working still stands, and does not depend on the defective metric: `docker_buildkit_cache_used_percent` is a direct ratio against its own budget, pinned at 99-100%, which is a bound holding rather than a difference of incommensurable units.\n- The root-filesystem trajectory (48.6% -> 70%) stands — it is a direct filesystem measurement.\n\nSo the epic's honest position is narrower than I stated: root is definitely filling, the BuildKit cap is definitely holding, and the ATTRIBUTION of the remaining growth is still open. A diagnosis is in flight and I have passed the same correction to it, along with that bug's genuinely useful finding — 20 `overlay2` directories referenced by neither cache-id nor mount-id, totalling 1,012,375,552 bytes, though with 2026-09-02 mtimes, so static and not the current producer.\n\nNone of this changes the reboot note above, which rests on S4's and S5's own mechanism findings rather than on any metric."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788599139786874277,"uuid":"5850e564-0d54-415e-8c9d-41babd18ce3c"}